IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coq-common`

-- Coroners Act 2003 (Queensland) -- part 3 division 3 (inquests and findings) as the family meets it: must an inquest
-- be held (ss 27-29), applying for one and challenging a refusal (s 30), the right to appear (s 36), a witness's
-- incriminating evidence and a later claim (s 39), the findings and comments and who gets them (ss 45, 46, 46A),
-- setting a finding aside (s 50), and whether the findings are evidence in a claim (ss 45(5), 46(3), 51(2)).
--
-- SEAT: a family member of the person who died, or another person with a sufficient interest. See NOTES.md.
--
-- One regulative rule: s 30(3), the coroner's duty to decide an application for an inquest within the prescribed
-- period, run by EVALTRACE inside exported questions that take only plain facts.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Coroners Act 2003: the inquest and the findings`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 27, 28, 29 -- must there be an inquest`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 27(1): "The coroner investigating a death must hold an inquest if-- (a) the coroner considers the death is-- (i) a
-- death in custody; or (ii) a death in care, in circumstances that raise issues about the deceased person's care; or
-- (iii) a death mentioned in section 8(3)(h) that is not also a death in custody, unless the coroner is satisfied the
-- circumstances of the death do not require the holding of an inquest; or (iv) a mining related reportable death; or (b)
-- the Attorney-General directs ...; or (c) the State Coroner, on the State Coroner's own initiative or on an application
-- under section 30, orders an inquest ...; or (d) the District Court, on an application under section 30, orders an
-- inquest". (2)(b): a death in custody does not include a death while detained under the Public Health Act 2005.
-- s 28(1): "An inquest may be held into a reportable death if the coroner investigating the death is satisfied it is in
-- the public interest". s 29: if someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be
-- in issue, an inquest must not start until the proceedings (and any appeal) end; one already started must be
-- adjourned. s 29 is read to override s 27 in time only ("must not start ... until"): the inquest is deferred, not
-- excused (fork F3).
DECLARE `The coroner's view of the death` IS ONE OF
    `a death in custody (not while detained under the Public Health Act 2005)`
    `a death in care, in circumstances that raise issues about the care`
    `a death in the course of or as a result of police operations, not in custody`
    `a mining related reportable death`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Must there be an inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in custody (s 27(1)(a)(i))`
    `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in care that raises issues about the care (s 27(1)(a)(ii))`
    `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest into a death in police operations, not being satisfied the circumstances do not require one (s 27(1)(a)(iii))`
    `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest into a mining related reportable death (s 27(1)(a)(iv))`
    `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, as the Attorney-General, the State Coroner or the District Court has directed or ordered one (s 27(1)(b)-(d), (3))`
    `not yet: someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue; an inquest must not start until those proceedings, and any appeal, end (s 29(2))`
    `adjourned: someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue; the inquest must be adjourned, and may resume after those proceedings end (s 29(3))`
    `may be held: the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest (s 28(1))`
    `not required: the coroner need not hold an inquest; you may apply for one (s 30)`

GIVEN `the coroner's view` IS A `The coroner's view of the death`
      `the coroner is satisfied no inquest is required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an inquest was directed or ordered` IS A BOOLEAN
      `someone has been charged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the criminal proceedings have ended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the inquest has started` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must there be an inquest`
`ss 27, 28, 29 -- must there be an inquest` `the coroner's view` `the coroner is satisfied no inquest is required` `an inquest was directed or ordered` `someone has been charged` `the criminal proceedings have ended` `the inquest has started` `the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest` MEANS
    IF `someone has been charged` AND (NOT `the criminal proceedings have ended`)
    THEN IF `the inquest has started`
         THEN `adjourned: someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue; the inquest must be adjourned, and may resume after those proceedings end (s 29(3))`
         ELSE `not yet: someone has been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue; an inquest must not start until those proceedings, and any appeal, end (s 29(2))`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the coroner's view`
         WHEN `a death in custody (not while detained under the Public Health Act 2005)` THEN `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in custody (s 27(1)(a)(i))`
         WHEN `a death in care, in circumstances that raise issues about the care` THEN `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, considering the death a death in care that raises issues about the care (s 27(1)(a)(ii))`
         WHEN `a mining related reportable death` THEN `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest into a mining related reportable death (s 27(1)(a)(iv))`
         WHEN `a death in the course of or as a result of police operations, not in custody` THEN
            IF NOT `the coroner is satisfied no inquest is required`
            THEN `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest into a death in police operations, not being satisfied the circumstances do not require one (s 27(1)(a)(iii))`
            ELSE `otherwise`
         OTHERWISE `otherwise`
    WHERE
        `otherwise` MEANS
            IF `an inquest was directed or ordered`
            THEN `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest, as the Attorney-General, the State Coroner or the District Court has directed or ordered one (s 27(1)(b)-(d), (3))`
            ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest`
                 THEN `may be held: the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest (s 28(1))`
                 ELSE `not required: the coroner need not hold an inquest; you may apply for one (s 30)`

@export Must there be an inquest into the death, or can it be held, or must it wait for a criminal case (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) ss 27-29)?
GIVEN `the coroner's view` IS A `The coroner's view of the death` @desc What the coroner considers the death to be
      `the coroner is satisfied no inquest is required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death in police operations (not in custody): is the coroner satisfied the circumstances do not require an inquest?
      `an inquest was directed or ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Attorney-General directed, or the State Coroner or the District Court ordered, that an inquest be held?
      `someone has been charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has someone been charged with an offence in which whether they caused the death may be in issue?
      `the criminal proceedings have ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: have those proceedings, including any appeal started in time, ended?
      `the inquest has started` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the inquest already started?
      `the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the coroner satisfied it is in the public interest to hold an inquest (for example to help prevent similar deaths)?
GIVETH A `Must there be an inquest`
DECIDE `Must there be an inquest question` IS
    `ss 27, 28, 29 -- must there be an inquest` `the coroner's view` `the coroner is satisfied no inquest is required` `an inquest was directed or ordered` `someone has been charged` `the criminal proceedings have ended` `the inquest has started` `the coroner is satisfied an inquest is in the public interest`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 30(1)-(3), (9) -- my application for an inquest, and the coroner's deadline`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 30(1): "A person may apply to the coroner investigating a person's death to hold an inquest into the death." (2):
-- in writing, outlining why it is in the public interest. (3): "The coroner must, within the prescribed period, decide
-- the application and give written reasons for the decision to-- (a) the applicant; and (b) if the coroner is not the
-- State Coroner--the State Coroner." (9): "prescribed period ... means-- (a) 6 months after the coroner receives the
-- application; or (b) the longer period the coroner considers necessary to enable the coroner to obtain relevant
-- information for making the decision." The longer period is the coroner's judgment, an input (its end day).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Coronial party` `Coronial act`
`s 30(3) -- decide the inquest application within the prescribed period` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the coroner`
    MUST `decide the application for an inquest and give written reasons`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the coroner` BECAUSE "s 30(3): the coroner did not decide the application for an inquest, and give written reasons, within the prescribed period (6 months after receiving it, or the longer period the coroner considered necessary)"

GIVEN `the day the coroner received it` IS A DATE
      `the coroner needs longer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the end of the longer period` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 30(9) -- the end of the prescribed period` `the day the coroner received it` `the coroner needs longer` `the end of the longer period` MEANS
    IF `the coroner needs longer` AND (NOT (`the end of the longer period` `is on or before` `six months on`))
    THEN `the end of the longer period`
    ELSE `six months on`
    WHERE
        `six months on` MEANS 6 `months after` `the day the coroner received it`

@export I applied for an inquest. By what day must the coroner decide my application and give me written reasons (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 30(3), (9))?
GIVEN `the day the coroner received it` IS A DATE @desc The date the coroner received your written application (ISO 8601)
      `the coroner needs longer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner considered a longer period necessary to obtain relevant information for the decision?
      `the end of the longer period` IS A DATE @desc If so, the date that longer period ends (ISO 8601); otherwise any date
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day for the inquest decision question` IS
    `s 30(9) -- the end of the prescribed period` `the day the coroner received it` `the coroner needs longer` `the end of the longer period`

@export I applied for an inquest. Has the coroner decided it in time (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 30(3), (9))?
GIVEN `the day the coroner received it` IS A DATE @desc The date the coroner received your written application (ISO 8601)
      `the coroner needs longer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner considered a longer period necessary to obtain relevant information for the decision?
      `the end of the longer period` IS A DATE @desc If so, the date that longer period ends (ISO 8601); otherwise any date
      `the coroner has decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner decided the application and given you written reasons?
      `the day the coroner decided` IS A DATE @desc The date the coroner did so (ISO 8601); if not yet, any date
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Is the coroner's step in time`
DECIDE `Inquest decision in time question` IS
    `the state of` `decide the application for an inquest and give written reasons` `under` (`s 30(3) -- decide the inquest application within the prescribed period` (`the number of days from` `the day the coroner received it` `until` (`s 30(9) -- the end of the prescribed period` `the day the coroner received it` `the coroner needs longer` `the end of the longer period`))) `from` `the day the coroner received it` `done` `the coroner has decided` `on` `the day the coroner decided` `as at` `today`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 30(4)-(8) -- challenging a decision not to hold an inquest`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 30(4): "If the coroner decides not to hold an inquest, the person may apply for an order that an inquest be held to--
-- (a) if the coroner is not the State Coroner--the State Coroner; or (b) if the coroner is the State Coroner--the
-- District Court." (5): "The application must be made within 14 days after the person receives the written reasons for
-- the coroner's decision." (6): "If the State Coroner refuses an application, the person may apply to the District
-- Court." (7): "The application must be made within 14 days after the person receives the written reasons for the State
-- Coroner's decision." (8): the State Coroner or District Court may order an inquest if satisfied it is in the public
-- interest. A period the text fixes: outside it, the answer is no (ruling of 2026-11-05 (a)).
DECLARE `Who refused the inquest` IS ONE OF
    `the investigating coroner, who is not the State Coroner`
    `the State Coroner, as the investigating coroner`
    `the State Coroner, refusing my application after the investigating coroner's refusal`

DECLARE `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: apply to the State Coroner for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(a), (5))`
    `yes: apply to the District Court for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(b), (5))`
    `yes: apply to the District Court; you are within the 14 days after receiving the State Coroner's written reasons (s 30(6), (7))`
    `no: the application must be made within 14 days after you receive the written reasons, and that period has ended (s 30(5) or (7))`

GIVEN `who refused` IS A `Who refused the inquest`
      `the day I received the written reasons` IS A DATE
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest`
`s 30(4)-(7) -- challenging the refusal of an inquest` `who refused` `the day I received the written reasons` `the day I apply` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the day I apply` `is on or before` (`the last day of the 14 days after` `the day I received the written reasons`))
    THEN `no: the application must be made within 14 days after you receive the written reasons, and that period has ended (s 30(5) or (7))`
    ELSE CONSIDER `who refused`
         WHEN `the investigating coroner, who is not the State Coroner` THEN `yes: apply to the State Coroner for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(a), (5))`
         WHEN `the State Coroner, as the investigating coroner` THEN `yes: apply to the District Court for an order that an inquest be held; you are within the 14 days after receiving the written reasons (s 30(4)(b), (5))`
         WHEN `the State Coroner, refusing my application after the investigating coroner's refusal` THEN `yes: apply to the District Court; you are within the 14 days after receiving the State Coroner's written reasons (s 30(6), (7))`

@export The coroner (or the State Coroner) decided not to hold an inquest. To whom can I apply for an order that one be held, and am I in time (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 30(4)-(7))?
GIVEN `who refused` IS A `Who refused the inquest` @desc Whose refusal you are challenging
      `the day I received the written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the written reasons for that refusal (ISO 8601)
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The date you apply, or will apply (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest`
DECIDE `Can I challenge the refusal of an inquest question` IS
    `s 30(4)-(7) -- challenging the refusal of an inquest` `who refused` `the day I received the written reasons` `the day I apply`

@export The coroner (or the State Coroner) refused an inquest. What is the last day to apply for an order that one be held (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 30(5), (7))?
GIVEN `the day I received the written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the written reasons for the refusal (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day to challenge the refusal of an inquest question` IS
    `the last day of the 14 days after` `the day I received the written reasons`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 36 -- appearing at the inquest`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 36(1): "The following persons may appear, examine witnesses, and make submissions, at an inquest-- (a) a police
-- officer, lawyer or other person assisting the Coroners Court; (b) the Attorney-General; (c) a person who the Coroners
-- Court considers has a sufficient interest in the inquest." Examples for (c): "1 a family member". (2), (3): a person
-- with a sufficient interest only because it is in the public interest that they make submissions about a matter for
-- comment under s 46(1) may not examine witnesses without leave and may only make submissions about such a matter. (4):
-- such a person may be represented by a lawyer. "Examine includes cross-examine." The list in (1) is not closed with
-- "only": for a person outside it the answer is "not covered" (NOTHING kind) (fork F4).
DECLARE `Can I take part in the inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may appear, examine and cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions, and may be represented by a lawyer (s 36(1)(c), (4))`
    `in part: you may appear and be represented by a lawyer, but may examine witnesses only with the court's leave and make submissions only about matters the coroner may comment on (s 36(2), (3), (4))`
    `in part, with leave: you may appear, examine witnesses (with the leave you have) and make submissions about matters the coroner may comment on, and be represented by a lawyer (s 36(2), (3), (4))`
    `not covered: s 36(1) gives the right only to those it lists; the Coroners Court has not considered you to have a sufficient interest, and the Act does not say whether you may take part otherwise`

GIVEN `the court considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `only because of the public interest in my submissions` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court gave me leave to examine witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I take part in the inquest`
`s 36 -- taking part in the inquest` `the court considers I have a sufficient interest` `only because of the public interest in my submissions` `the court gave me leave to examine witnesses` MEANS
    IF NOT `the court considers I have a sufficient interest`
    THEN `not covered: s 36(1) gives the right only to those it lists; the Coroners Court has not considered you to have a sufficient interest, and the Act does not say whether you may take part otherwise`
    ELSE IF `only because of the public interest in my submissions`
         THEN IF `the court gave me leave to examine witnesses`
              THEN `in part, with leave: you may appear, examine witnesses (with the leave you have) and make submissions about matters the coroner may comment on, and be represented by a lawyer (s 36(2), (3), (4))`
              ELSE `in part: you may appear and be represented by a lawyer, but may examine witnesses only with the court's leave and make submissions only about matters the coroner may comment on (s 36(2), (3), (4))`
         ELSE `yes: you may appear, examine and cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions, and may be represented by a lawyer (s 36(1)(c), (4))`

@export Can I appear at the inquest, question witnesses, make submissions and have a lawyer (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 36)?
GIVEN `the court considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Coroners Court consider you have a sufficient interest in the inquest (a family member usually will)?
      `only because of the public interest in my submissions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your interest recognised only because it is in the public interest that you make submissions on matters the coroner may comment on (for example as an advocacy group)?
      `the court gave me leave to examine witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: has the court given you leave to examine witnesses?
GIVETH A `Can I take part in the inquest`
DECIDE `Can I take part in the inquest question` IS
    `s 36 -- taking part in the inquest` `the court considers I have a sufficient interest` `only because of the public interest in my submissions` `the court gave me leave to examine witnesses`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 39 -- a witness's incriminating evidence, and my later claim`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 39(1): "This section applies if a witness refuses to give oral evidence at an inquest because the evidence would tend
-- to incriminate the person." (2): the coroner may require it if satisfied it is in the public interest. (3): "The
-- evidence is not admissible against the witness in any other proceeding, other than a proceeding for perjury." (4):
-- "Derivative evidence is not admissible against the witness in a criminal proceeding." "Derivative evidence means any
-- information, document or other evidence obtained as a direct or indirect result of the evidence given by the witness."
-- s 39 is silent on derivative evidence in a civil proceeding: "not covered" (NOTHING kind).
DECLARE `Kind of later proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a civil claim (for example for compensation or damages)`
    `a criminal proceeding other than for perjury`
    `a proceeding for perjury`

DECLARE `Kind of evidence` IS ONE OF
    `the evidence the witness was required to give`
    `derivative evidence (anything found as a result of that evidence)`

DECLARE `Can the witness's evidence be used against them` IS ONE OF
    `no: the evidence the witness was required to give is not admissible against the witness in any other proceeding (s 39(3))`
    `yes: s 39(3) excepts a proceeding for perjury`
    `no: derivative evidence is not admissible against the witness in a criminal proceeding (s 39(4))`
    `not covered: s 39 does not bar derivative evidence in a civil proceeding, and the Act says nothing more`
    `s 39 gives no protection on these facts: it applies only where the witness refused to give oral evidence because it would tend to incriminate them and the coroner required it`

GIVEN `the witness refused and was required to answer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the evidence` IS A `Kind of evidence`
      `the proceeding` IS A `Kind of later proceeding`
GIVETH A `Can the witness's evidence be used against them`
`s 39 -- incriminating evidence in a later proceeding` `the witness refused and was required to answer` `the evidence` `the proceeding` MEANS
    IF NOT `the witness refused and was required to answer`
    THEN `s 39 gives no protection on these facts: it applies only where the witness refused to give oral evidence because it would tend to incriminate them and the coroner required it`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the evidence`
         WHEN `the evidence the witness was required to give` THEN
            CONSIDER `the proceeding`
            WHEN `a proceeding for perjury` THEN `yes: s 39(3) excepts a proceeding for perjury`
            OTHERWISE `no: the evidence the witness was required to give is not admissible against the witness in any other proceeding (s 39(3))`
         WHEN `derivative evidence (anything found as a result of that evidence)` THEN
            CONSIDER `the proceeding`
            WHEN `a civil claim (for example for compensation or damages)` THEN `not covered: s 39 does not bar derivative evidence in a civil proceeding, and the Act says nothing more`
            OTHERWISE `no: derivative evidence is not admissible against the witness in a criminal proceeding (s 39(4))`

@export A witness at the inquest was made to give evidence that tends to incriminate them. Can that evidence, or what it led to, be used against them in my later claim or another case (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 39)?
GIVEN `the witness refused and was required to answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the witness refuse to give oral evidence because it would tend to incriminate them, and the coroner then require them to give it?
      `the evidence` IS A `Kind of evidence` @desc The evidence you want to use: what the witness said, or something found as a result of it
      `the proceeding` IS A `Kind of later proceeding` @desc The later proceeding in which it would be used against the witness
GIVETH A `Can the witness's evidence be used against them`
DECIDE `Can the witness's evidence be used against them question` IS
    `s 39 -- incriminating evidence in a later proceeding` `the witness refused and was required to answer` `the evidence` `the proceeding`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 45(4), 46(2), 46A -- getting the findings and comments`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 45(4): "The coroner must give a written copy of the findings to-- (a) a family member of the deceased person who has
-- indicated that he or she will accept the document for the deceased person's family; and (b) if an inquest was
-- held--any person who, as a person with a sufficient interest in the inquest, appeared at the inquest; ...". (7): "This
-- section applies whether or not an inquest is held." s 46(1): comments are made on a death "investigated at an
-- inquest"; (2): the copy goes to the same (a) and (b) persons. The recipient lists are the whole of each duty: a person
-- outside them is told "the section does not require it" (fork F8). s 45(3): the findings need not be made if the
-- coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2).
DECLARE `Must I be given the findings` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings and of any comments (ss 45(4), 46(2))`
    `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings (s 45(4)); comments are made only on deaths investigated at an inquest (s 46(1))`
    `no findings need be made: the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2) (s 45(3)(b))`
    `ss 45(4) and 46(2) do not require that you be given a copy: they name the family member who agreed to accept it for the family, and those who appeared at an inquest with a sufficient interest`

GIVEN `the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am the family member accepting documents for the family` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an inquest was held` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I appeared at the inquest with a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must I be given the findings`
`ss 45(4), 46(2) -- copies of the findings and comments` `the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2)` `I am the family member accepting documents for the family` `an inquest was held` `I appeared at the inquest with a sufficient interest` MEANS
    IF `the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2)`
    THEN `no findings need be made: the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2) (s 45(3)(b))`
    ELSE IF `I am the family member accepting documents for the family` OR (`an inquest was held` AND `I appeared at the inquest with a sufficient interest`)
         THEN IF `an inquest was held`
              THEN `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings and of any comments (ss 45(4), 46(2))`
              ELSE `yes: you must be given a written copy of the findings (s 45(4)); comments are made only on deaths investigated at an inquest (s 46(1))`
         ELSE `ss 45(4) and 46(2) do not require that you be given a copy: they name the family member who agreed to accept it for the family, and those who appeared at an inquest with a sufficient interest`

@export Must I be given a written copy of the coroner's findings and comments (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) ss 45(4), 46(2))?
GIVEN `the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the coroner stop investigating the death (for example because a doctor was authorised to issue a cause of death certificate)?
      `I am the family member accepting documents for the family` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the family member of the person who died who has told the coroner you will accept documents for the family?
      `an inquest was held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an inquest held?
      `I appeared at the inquest with a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you appear at the inquest as a person with a sufficient interest?
GIVETH A `Must I be given the findings`
DECIDE `Must I be given the findings question` IS
    `ss 45(4), 46(2) -- copies of the findings and comments` `the coroner stopped investigating under s 12(2)` `I am the family member accepting documents for the family` `an inquest was held` `I appeared at the inquest with a sufficient interest`

-- s 46A(1): "If a coroner investigates a death at an inquest, the coroner must publish the following on the State
-- Coroner's website unless the coroner orders otherwise-- (a) the coroner's findings; (b) ... the coroner's comments."
-- (2): "If a coroner investigated a death but did not hold an inquest, the coroner may direct that the coroner's findings
-- be published on the State Coroner's website only if-- (a) the coroner considers the publication is in the public
-- interest; and (b) to the extent practicable, the coroner has consulted with and had regard to the views of a family
-- member of the deceased person."
DECLARE `Will the findings be published` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the findings, and any comments, must be published on the State Coroner's website (s 46A(1))`
    `no: the coroner has ordered that the inquest findings not be published (s 46A(1))`
    `possibly: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication, considering it in the public interest and having consulted a family member as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`
    `no: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication only if it is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`

GIVEN `an inquest was held` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner considers publication in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a family member was consulted as far as practicable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Will the findings be published`
`s 46A -- publication of the findings` `an inquest was held` `the coroner ordered otherwise` `the coroner considers publication in the public interest` `a family member was consulted as far as practicable` MEANS
    IF `an inquest was held`
    THEN IF `the coroner ordered otherwise`
         THEN `no: the coroner has ordered that the inquest findings not be published (s 46A(1))`
         ELSE `yes: the findings, and any comments, must be published on the State Coroner's website (s 46A(1))`
    ELSE IF `the coroner considers publication in the public interest` AND `a family member was consulted as far as practicable`
         THEN `possibly: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication, considering it in the public interest and having consulted a family member as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`
         ELSE `no: without an inquest the coroner may direct publication only if it is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 46A(2))`

@export Will the coroner's findings be published on the State Coroner's website (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 46A)?
GIVEN `an inquest was held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the death investigated at an inquest?
      `the coroner ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there was an inquest: has the coroner ordered that the findings not be published?
      `the coroner considers publication in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there was no inquest: does the coroner consider publication is in the public interest?
      `a family member was consulted as far as practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there was no inquest: has the coroner, as far as practicable, consulted a family member and had regard to their views?
GIVETH A `Will the findings be published`
DECIDE `Will the findings be published question` IS
    `s 46A -- publication of the findings` `an inquest was held` `the coroner ordered otherwise` `the coroner considers publication in the public interest` `a family member was consulted as far as practicable`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 45(5), 46(3), 51(2) -- the findings and a claim for compensation`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 45(5): "The coroner must not include in the findings any statement that a person is, or may be-- (a) guilty of an
-- offence; or (b) civilly liable for something." s 46(3): the same for comments. s 51(2): "The record of the coroner's
-- findings and comments is not evidence in any court or tribunal of any fact asserted in the record." Two flat
-- negatives. Using the record only to show that a finding was made is not a use s 51(2) bars: "not covered".
DECLARE `How I want to use the findings` IS ONE OF
    `as evidence in a court or tribunal of a fact the findings or comments state`
    `to show in a court or tribunal only that the findings or comments were made`

DECLARE `Can the findings help my claim` IS ONE OF
    `no: the record of the findings and comments is not evidence in any court or tribunal of any fact asserted in it (s 51(2)); and they must not state that anyone is or may be guilty of an offence or civilly liable (ss 45(5), 46(3))`
    `not covered: s 51(2) bars the record only as evidence of the facts asserted in it; the findings still must not state that anyone is or may be guilty or civilly liable (ss 45(5), 46(3))`

GIVEN `the use` IS A `How I want to use the findings`
GIVETH A `Can the findings help my claim`
`ss 45(5), 46(3), 51(2) -- the findings in a claim` `the use` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the use`
    WHEN `as evidence in a court or tribunal of a fact the findings or comments state` THEN `no: the record of the findings and comments is not evidence in any court or tribunal of any fact asserted in it (s 51(2)); and they must not state that anyone is or may be guilty of an offence or civilly liable (ss 45(5), 46(3))`
    WHEN `to show in a court or tribunal only that the findings or comments were made` THEN `not covered: s 51(2) bars the record only as evidence of the facts asserted in it; the findings still must not state that anyone is or may be guilty or civilly liable (ss 45(5), 46(3))`

@export Can I use the coroner's findings as evidence in my compensation claim, and will they say who is to blame (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) ss 45(5), 46(3), 51(2))?
GIVEN `the use` IS A `How I want to use the findings` @desc How you want to use the findings in a court or tribunal
GIVETH A `Can the findings help my claim`
DECIDE `Can the findings help my claim question` IS
    `ss 45(5), 46(3), 51(2) -- the findings in a claim` `the use`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 50 -- setting aside a finding at an inquest`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 50(1): "A person dissatisfied with a finding at an inquest may apply to the State Coroner or District Court to set
-- aside the finding." (2): to the District Court even if the State Coroner has refused on the same grounds. (3):
-- "However, the person may not apply to the State Coroner if, on an application based on the same or substantially the
-- same grounds or evidence, the District Court has refused to set aside the finding." (4): the State Coroner may set
-- aside if satisfied "(a) new evidence casts doubt on the finding; or (b) the finding was not correctly recorded." (5):
-- the District Court on those grounds and "(c) there was no evidence to support the finding; or (d) the finding could not
-- be reasonably supported by the evidence." No time limit is set (finding 6). A finding without an inquest is outside
-- s 50; s 50B lets the State Coroner or the coroner reopen on their own initiative, with no application (not covered).
DECLARE `My ground for setting aside the finding` IS ONE OF
    `new evidence casts doubt on the finding`
    `the finding was not correctly recorded`
    `there was no evidence to support the finding`
    `the finding could not reasonably be supported by the evidence`

DECLARE `Can I have the finding set aside` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the State Coroner or the District Court; both may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(1), (4), (5)); s 50 sets no time limit`
    `yes, to the District Court: only the District Court may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(5)(c), (d)); s 50 sets no time limit`
    `yes, to the District Court only: the District Court has refused on the same or substantially the same grounds, so you may not apply to the State Coroner (s 50(3)); s 50 sets no time limit`
    `not covered: s 50 is for findings at an inquest; without an inquest the Act gives no application, though the State Coroner or the coroner may reopen the investigation on their own initiative (s 50B)`

GIVEN `the finding was made at an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my ground` IS A `My ground for setting aside the finding`
      `the District Court refused on the same grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I have the finding set aside`
`s 50 -- setting aside a finding` `the finding was made at an inquest` `my ground` `the District Court refused on the same grounds` MEANS
    IF NOT `the finding was made at an inquest`
    THEN `not covered: s 50 is for findings at an inquest; without an inquest the Act gives no application, though the State Coroner or the coroner may reopen the investigation on their own initiative (s 50B)`
    ELSE IF `the District Court refused on the same grounds`
         THEN `yes, to the District Court only: the District Court has refused on the same or substantially the same grounds, so you may not apply to the State Coroner (s 50(3)); s 50 sets no time limit`
         ELSE IF `a State Coroner ground`
              THEN `yes: you may apply to the State Coroner or the District Court; both may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(1), (4), (5)); s 50 sets no time limit`
              ELSE `yes, to the District Court: only the District Court may set aside a finding on your ground (s 50(5)(c), (d)); s 50 sets no time limit`
    WHERE
        `a State Coroner ground` MEANS
            CONSIDER `my ground`
            WHEN `new evidence casts doubt on the finding` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `the finding was not correctly recorded` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

@export I am dissatisfied with a coroner's finding. Can I apply to have it set aside, and to whom (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 50)?
GIVEN `the finding was made at an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the finding made at an inquest (rather than after an investigation without an inquest)?
      `my ground` IS A `My ground for setting aside the finding` @desc Why you say the finding should be set aside
      `the District Court refused on the same grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the District Court already refused to set the finding aside on the same or substantially the same grounds or evidence?
GIVETH A `Can I have the finding set aside`
DECIDE `Can I have the finding set aside question` IS
    `s 50 -- setting aside a finding` `the finding was made at an inquest` `my ground` `the District Court refused on the same grounds`
